\v 4 I'll put¦346081 hooks¦346082 into¦346084 your jaw¦346084, and¦346081 the fish from your streams will¦346081 cling to the scales on¦346084 your \add side\add*. I'll lift you¦346090 up¦346090 from¦346091 the middle of¦346091 your river, along with¦346084 all¦346094 the¦346096 river's fish¦346096 that cling to your scales.
\v 5 I'll fling¦346101 you¦346101 into¦346102 the¦346102 wilderness¦346102—you¦346103 and¦346104 all¦346105 the¦346107 fish¦346107 from your¦346108 river. You'll fall¦346113 on¦346109 the open countryside¦346112—\add @your¦346101 corpse¦346101\add* won't¦346114 be¦346115 taken or moved, \add but rather\add* I'll give¦346122 you¦346122 as food¦346123 to¦346118 the birds¦346120 and wild animals¦346118.
\v 6 Then¦346125 all¦346126 of Egypt's¦346129 inhabitants¦346128 will¦346125 know¦346125 that¦346130 I¦346131 am¦346132 Yahweh¦346132, because¦346133 they've¦346134 been¦346134 \add like a brittle\add* reed¦346136 to the¦346137 \add ≈kingdom\add* of¦346137 Yisrael¦346138.\x + \xo 29:6: \xt Isa 36:6.\x*
\v 7 When¦346140 \add @Yisrael¦346140\add* \add ≈grabbed¦346140 you¦346141\add*, you¦346144 broke and¦346145 tore open their shoulder¦346149, and¦346150 when¦346150 they¦346150 leaned¦346150 on¦346141 you, you broke¦346145 and¦346153 made¦346153 their legs unsteady¦346153.
