\p
\v 28 Then¦352006 the man brought¦352006 me¦352006 to¦352007 the¦352010 inner¦352010 courtyard¦352009 by¦352011 way of¦352011 its southern¦352012 gate¦352016, which had the same measurements¦352013 as the other gates.
\v 29 Its¦352021 chambers¦352021, walls, and porches¦352025 measured¦352027 the same as the other gates—there were¦352027 windows¦352029 all¦352033 around¦352033 in the¦352038 porch. The¦352039 inner gate with its¦352023 porch¦352031 \add ≈was¦352040 twenty-seven¦352040 metres long¦352038 and¦352039 thirteen metres wide¦352039\add*.
\v 30 There were¦352045 also¦352050 porches¦352044 all¦352045 around¦352045 the¦352048 inner wall—they were \add all\add* \add ≈thirteen metres long¦352048 and¦352044 almost three metres wide¦352052\add*.
\v 31 The¦352061 porch¦352056 facing the¦352061 outer¦352061 courtyard¦352060 had \add carved\add* palm¦352062 trees¦352062 on its¦352065 walls, and¦352056 eight¦352068 steps¦352067 going up to¦352058 it.
\s1 The eastern gate to the inner courtyard
\rem /s1 The Inner Courtyard: The East Gate
\p
\v 32 Then¦352072 the man brought¦352072 me¦352072 to¦352073 the inner¦352076 courtyard¦352075 by the eastern¦352078 side and¦352079 measured¦352079 the¦352082 gate¦352082, which had the¦352083 same measurements¦352083 as the other gates¦352082.
\v 33 Its¦352086 chambers¦352086, walls, and porches¦352090 measured¦352092 the same as the other gates, and there were¦352092 windows¦352094 all¦352098 around¦352098. The¦352101 inner gate with its¦352088 porch¦352096 \add ≈was¦352102 twenty-seven¦352106 metres long¦352101 and¦352104 thirteen metres wide¦352104\add*.
\v 34 Its¦352109 porch¦352109 \add that\add* faced¦352111 the¦352112 outer¦352112 courtyard¦352111 had \add carved\add* palm¦352113 trees¦352113 on each side of¦352109 it, and¦352109 eight¦352120 steps¦352121 going up it.
\s1 The northern gate to the inner courtyard
\rem /s1 The Inner Courtyard: The North Gate
\p
\v 35 Next the man brought¦352125 me¦352125 to¦352126 the northern¦352129 gate¦352128 and measured it, \add and\add* it measured the same as the other gates¦352128.
\v 36 Its¦352134 chambers¦352134, walls, and¦352138 porch measured the same as the other gates, and¦352140 there were windows¦352140 all¦352142 around¦352142. The¦352145 gateway with its¦352136 porch \add ≈was¦352146 twenty-seven¦352150 metres long¦352145 and¦352148 thirteen metres wide¦352148\add*.
\v 37 Its¦352153 porch \add that\add* faced¦352155 the¦352156 outer¦352156 courtyard¦352155 had \add carved\add* palm¦352157 trees¦352157 on each side of¦352153 it, and¦352153 eight¦352164 steps¦352165 going up it.
\s1 Rooms for preparing sacrifices
\rem /s1 Rooms for Preparing Sacrifices; Buildings Near the North Gate; The Rooms for Preparing Sacrifices
\p
\v 38 There¦352173 was a room¦352169 with¦352171 a door¦352170 by each of¦352170 the¦352172 inner gateways¦352172 which was where they rinsed¦352174 the¦352177 burnt¦352177 offerings¦352177.
\v 39 There were¦352179 two¦352181 tables¦352182 on¦352179 each side of¦352179 each porch¦352179, which¦352188 were for slaughtering¦352187 \add the offerings:\add* the¦352189 burnt¦352189 offering¦352189, the¦352190 sin¦352190 offering, and¦352179 the guilt¦352191 offering¦352191.
\v 40 By the wall of¦352198 the courtyard, going¦352197 up¦352197 to¦352198 the gate to the north¦352200, there were two tables. Also¦352193 on the other¦352206 side¦352205 there were two tables at the gate's¦352199 porch¦352208.
\v 41 There were¦352219 four¦352216 tables¦352217 by the¦352219 gate¦352220 on each side¦352219—they slaughtered¦352224 animals on those eight¦352221 tables¦352222.
\v 42 There were¦352229 four¦352226 cut-stone tables¦352227 for¦352228 the¦352231 burnt offerings, \add ≈each just under a metre long¦352231 and wide¦352235, and¦352238 standing half¦352238 a metre tall\add*. They kept the tools which¦352247 they used to¦352242 slaughter¦352248 the¦352251 burnt¦352251 offerings¦352251 for the¦352253 sacrifices¦352253 on¦352252 those¦352252 \add stone\add* tables.
\v 43 Some two-pronged hooks¦352255 a¦352256 handbreadth in length were¦352258 fastened all¦352260 around¦352260 in the porch, and¦352255 the flesh¦352266 of¦352266 the¦352267 offerings¦352267 would be¦352258 put on¦352259 the¦352265 tables¦352265.
\s1 The priests' rooms
\rem /s1 Rooms for the Priests
\p
\v 44 Within the inner¦352271 courtyard¦352274 \add but\add* on the outside¦352269 of¦352270 the inner¦352275 gate¦352280 were¦352274 singers¦352273' rooms¦352272—one on the northern¦352281 side facing south¦352284, and¦352269 the other on the southern¦352284 side¦352288 facing north¦352293.
\v 45 Then¦352295 the man told¦352295 me¦352296, “This¦352297 room¦352298 facing¦352300 south¦352302 is¦352301 for¦352303 the priests¦352303 who¦352304 are on duty in the temple¦352306.
\v 46 The¦352308 room¦352308 facing¦352310 north¦352312 is¦352313 for the priests¦352313 on duty at the altar¦352316, that¦352309 is¦352311, for Tsadok's¦352320 descendants—they're¦352317 the \add only\add* Levites¦352324 who¦352314 approach¦352321 Yahweh¦352327 to¦352325 serve¦352328 him¦352328.”
\s1 The inner courtyard
\rem /s1 The Inner Courtyard and the Temple Building; The Inner Courtyard and Temple
\p
\v 47 Next he¦352330 measured¦352330 the square¦352341 courtyard¦352333 at fifty-four metres long¦352334 and¦352330 wide¦352338, with the altar¦352342 in¦352343 front¦352343 of¦352343 the¦352344 temple¦352344.
\s1 The temple entrance
\rem /s1 The Temple
\p
\v 48 Then¦352346 the man took¦352346 me¦352346 to¦352347 the temple¦352350 porch¦352349 and¦352351 measured¦352351 its doorposts—they were each two and a half metres thick. The¦352360 entryway itself was¦352362 seven metres¦352355 wide,\f + \fr 40:48 \ft Following the LXX, as the 1.5m (three cubits) in the Hebrew appears likely to be a copying mistake.\f* and the walls on each side of¦352360 it were one and¦352365 a half metres wide.
\v 49 The¦352370 porch¦352370 was¦352371 eleven metres wide¦352373 and¦352377 six metres deep, \add with\add* steps¦352377 going¦352379 up¦352379 to¦352380 it and¦352381 pillars¦352381 standing on¦352377 each side of¦352369 it.
\c 41
\s1 Inside the temple
\p
\v 1 Then¦352390 the man took¦352390 me¦352390 into¦352391 the¦352393 temple's¦352393 holy place and¦352390 measured¦352394 the¦352397 doorposts \add at\add* three metres¦352400 thick on each side.
\v 2 The¦352413 doorway was five¦352419 metres wide \add and\add* the wall on each side¦352417 was two and a half metres long. Then¦352413 the man measured¦352425 the dimensions of¦352426 the¦352429 holy place—twenty-one¦352430 metres long and eleven metres wide.
\v 3 Then¦352433 the man went¦352433 into¦352434 the \add very sacred\add* inner¦352434 place and¦352435 measured¦352435 the door at one metre wide, and the doorway was¦352446 three metres wide. The¦352436 walls on either side were¦352442 nearly four metres wide.
\v 4 Then he¦352449 measured¦352449 the square room's length¦352452 and¦352449 width¦352455 of¦352452 eleven metres, \add measuring\add* to¦352458 the temple¦352461 hallway. Then he¦352462 said¦352462 to¦352463 me¦352463, “This¦352464 is¦352465 the¦352465 most sacred¦352465 area.”
\s1 The temple rooms
\rem /s1 The Rooms Built against the Temple Walls
\p
\v 5 Then¦352468 the man measured¦352468 the¦352469 wall¦352469 of¦352469 the¦352471 house¦352471 at three metres thick. The¦352474 width¦352474 of¦352474 each¦352478 side room around¦352478 the house was¦352476 two metres.
\v 6 There were¦352489 side rooms on three¦352489 levels, one room above another, thirty¦352490 rooms on each¦352498 level. There were ledges¦352492 around¦352498 the temple wall to¦352496 support¦352502 all¦352498 the side rooms, as the supports¦352506 weren't built into¦352507 the¦352507 temple wall¦352507.
\v 7 So¦352510 the side rooms widened and¦352511 went around going up, because¦352515 the house¦352518 went around¦352521 higher and higher all¦352523 around—the rooms widened \add ≈on each¦352521 higher level\add*, and¦352532 a stairway went up through the¦352533 middle¦352538 level to¦352535 the¦352537 highest level.
\v 8 Then¦352540 I saw¦352540 a raised part all¦352543 around¦352543 the temple¦352541, the¦352546 foundation¦352546 for the¦352548 side rooms¦352548. It measured a full rod in height¦352542—\add ≈just over three metres¦352552\add*.
\rem /s1 The Building on the West
\v 9 The¦352556 width¦352555 of¦352555 the wall¦352556 of the side rooms on the outside¦352562 was¦352563 two and¦352565 a half metres¦352564. There was¦352566 an open space¦352566 outside those rooms in the¦352568 temple¦352570.
\v 10 On the other side of¦352574 that open space were¦352575 the priests' outer side rooms¦352573. That space was¦352575 eleven metres wide¦352574 all¦352579 around¦352579 the temple¦352578.
\v 11 There were¦352585 doors into¦352585 the side rooms from another open space¦352585 with one¦352587 doorway¦352590 on the north¦352589 side, and¦352583 the other on the south¦352592. The¦352594 width¦352593 of¦352594 that open area was¦352596 two and a half metres¦352597 all¦352598 around¦352598.
\s1 The western side
\p
\v 12 The¦352602 building that¦352603 faced¦352606 the¦352607 courtyard on the¦352608 western side¦352608 was¦352613 thirty-six metres wide. Its¦352624 wall¦352615 was¦352617 two and a half metres thick all¦352621 around¦352621, and¦352602 it was¦352604 forty-seven metres long¦352624.
\s1 The temple building measurements
\rem /s1 The Total Measurements of the Temple Building
\p
\v 13 Then¦352628 the man measured¦352628 the temple¦352631 as being fifty-four metres long. The¦352632 separate¦352635 building¦352636, its¦352637 wall¦352637, and¦352628 the courtyard \add combined\add* also¦352635 measured fifty-four metres \add ≈across\add*.
\v 14 The¦352642 width¦352642 of the front of the courtyard in front of the temple¦352644 was¦352647 also¦352645 fifty-four metres.
\p
\v 15 Then¦352650 the man measured¦352650 the¦352651 length¦352651 of¦352656 the building¦352653 behind¦352661 the temple¦352668, to its¦352662 west, and¦352670 including¦352662 the galleries¦352662 on either side, it¦352661 came to fifty-four metres.
\rem /s1 Details of the Temple Building
\p The¦352669 sacred inner¦352669 place¦352657 and the¦352668 porch¦352670,
\v 16 the¦352673 inner walls and¦352674 the¦352674 windows, including¦352674 the¦352675 narrow¦352675 windows, and¦352676 the¦352676 galleries¦352676 all¦352678 around¦352678 on three¦352679 levels, were¦352692 all panelled¦352682 in wood¦352683.
\v 17 Above the¦352697 entryway to¦352698 the inner¦352701 sanctuary and¦352702 spaced along the¦352707 walls¦352707 there was a \add ≈repeating\add* pattern.
\v 18 It was¦352715 decorated with winged creatures and¦352717 palm¦352717 trees¦352717—with a palm¦352718 tree¦352718 between¦352719 each winged creature, and each winged creature had two¦352723 faces¦352724:
\v 19 the¦352727 human¦352728 face looked toward a palm tree on one side, and¦352727 the young¦352735 lion¦352735 face looked toward a palm¦352738 tree¦352738 on the¦352745 other side. They were carved all¦352746 around¦352746 the entire¦352743 temple¦352745.
\v 20 From¦352750 the¦352750 ground¦352750 to¦352751 above the¦352754 doorway¦352754, winged creatures and¦352756 palm¦352756 trees¦352756 were¦352757 carved on the¦352758 outer wall¦352758 of¦352758 the¦352759 temple¦352759.
\v 21 The¦352762 door posts of¦352763 the \add ≈main temple¦352762 room¦352766\add* were¦352768 square¦352764, all with the same appearance¦352767.
\rem /s1 The Wooden Altar
\v 22 The¦352787 wooden altar¦352770 was¦352771 one metre on each side¦352782 and stood one and a half metres¦352773 high¦352774. Its¦352779 corner¦352779 posts, base, and frame were¦352783 \add all\add* made of¦352775 wood¦352783.
\p Then the man said¦352784 to¦352785 me¦352785, “This¦352786 is¦352787 the table¦352787 that¦352788 stands \add ≈in¦352789 Yahweh's¦352790 presence¦352789\add*.”
\s1 The temple doorways
\rem /s1 The Doors
\p
\v 23 Both the main room¦352795 and¦352795 the sacred place¦352795 had double¦352792 doors¦352793—
\v 24 each consisting of¦352806 two¦352797 parts that swung on hinges.
\v 25 There were¦352819 carvings of¦352814 winged creatures and¦352817 palm¦352817 trees¦352817 on the doors¦352814, just¦352818 like¦352818 on the walls¦352820, and¦352821 there was¦352823 a wooden¦352822 roof over the¦352825 front¦352825 porch¦352826.
\v 26 There were narrow¦352830 windows¦352829 and¦352831 palm¦352831 trees¦352831 on either side of¦352836 the¦352837 porch¦352837, like the¦352838 side rooms¦352838 of¦352838 the¦352839 temple¦352839, and they also¦352831 had overhanging roofs.
\c 42
\s1 Two buildings near the temple
\rem /s1 Rooms for the Priests; The Holy Chambers and the Outer Wall; Two Buildings Near the Temple
\p
\v 1 Next the man sent me¦352842 out¦352842 to¦352843 the outer courtyard on the northern¦352849 side, and¦352850 he¦352850 brought¦352850 me¦352850 to¦352851 rooms¦352853 in front¦352855 of¦352848 the outer courtyard and¦352857 the northern¦352863 outer wall.
\v 2 That building with a north-facing¦352873 entrance¦352872 was¦352875 fifty-four¦352875 metres long¦352869 and¦352874 twenty-seven metres wide¦352874.
\v 3 Some of those rooms faced¦352891 the inner¦352882 courtyard and were eleven metres away from the sanctuary. There were three¦352894 levels of rooms, and the ones above looked down on¦352894 the ones below and were open to¦352889 them, having a walkway. Some of the rooms looked out onto the outer¦352887 courtyard.
\v 4 A five metre wide¦352901 passage¦352898 ran in front¦352896 of¦352896 the¦352897 rooms¦352897 and¦352908 was¦352896 fifty-four\f + \fr 42:4 \ft The Hebrew text seemed to be missing a word here (as it didn't make sense), so the word from the (approx. 300 B.C.) Septuagint translation was used.\f* metres¦352900 long. The¦352904 rooms' doors¦352908 \add ≈faced\add* north¦352909.
\v 5 But¦352911 the upper¦352912 halls were¦352913 smaller, because¦352914 the walkways \add ≈there used\add* more¦352920 space than¦352920 they¦352916 did in the bottom and¦352920 middle¦352920 levels of the¦352921 building.
\v 6 \add @The¦352929 third¦352929 storey¦352929 halls¦352929\add* had no pillars¦352929 (unlike the¦352930 courtyards¦352930), so¦352933 \add @the¦352935 rooms¦352935 at¦352935 the¦352935 highest¦352935 level¦352935\add* were¦352924 smaller than¦352936 those on the bottom and¦352926 middle¦352936 levels.
\v 7 The¦352944 outside¦352942 wall¦352939 ran along the rooms toward the outer¦352947 courtyard¦352946 that¦352940 was in front¦352950 of¦352945 the rooms¦352951, \add and\add* was twenty-seven metres long¦352952.
\v 8 The¦352965 \add row of\add* rooms along the outer¦352962 courtyard¦352961 was¦352963 twenty-seven metres long, and¦352965 the¦352968 \add row of\add* rooms facing¦352968 the sanctuary was fifty-four¦352963 metres long.
\v 9 There was an entrance to¦352981 the lowest rooms from the eastern¦352979 side, coming¦352980 from¦352982 the outer¦352983 courtyard¦352982.
\v 10 There was¦352985 \add also a set of\add* rooms¦352999 along the outer courtyard wall¦352987 on the eastern¦352990 side of the outer courtyard, in¦352985 front¦352993 of the sanctuary's inner courtyard,
\v 11 with a walkway in front¦353002 of¦353002 them¦353002. They \add ≈looked similar to\add* the rooms¦353004 on the northern¦353007 side and¦353011 had the same \add ≈measurements\add*, as well as the same exits¦353012 and¦353013 arrangements¦353013 and¦353014 doors¦353014.
\v 12 There were also¦353016 doorways into rooms on the south¦353020 side that¦353018 were similar¦353016 to¦353030 what was on the north side. There was an inside passage with¦353022 an outer door—the passage had doors¦353016 into all the rooms. At¦353022 the eastern¦353029 end of¦353028 the passage there was a door from the outside.
\p
\v 13 Then¦353032 the man told¦353032 me¦353033, “The¦353034 northern¦353035 rooms¦353036 and the¦353037 southern¦353037 rooms that¦353038 are¦353039 in front¦353041 of¦353041 the¦353042 outer courtyard¦353042 are¦353045 sacred rooms¦353045 where¦353047 the¦353051 priests¦353051 who¦353052 work nearest¦353054 to¦353055 Yahweh¦353055 may eat¦353048 the very sacred food. They'll put¦353059 the very sacred things¦353056 there¦353058—the food offering¦353063, the¦353064 sin¦353064 offering¦353064, and¦353065 the¦353065 guilt¦353065 offering¦353065—because¦353066 it's¦353032 a very sacred area.
\v 14 When¦353070 the¦353071 priests¦353071 enter¦353070 it, they¦353070 must not¦353072 go¦353074 \add back\add* out¦353074 of¦353082 the sacred area to¦353096 the outer¦353079 courtyard¦353078, without laying aside the¦353079 clothes¦353082 in which¦353083 they¦353085 served¦353085, since those \add ≡clothes\add* are¦353089 sacred. So they¦353090 must dress in other¦353094 clothes before going \add ≈back into¦353076 the¦353078 public areas\add*.”
\s1 Measuring outside the temple
\rem /s1 The Measurements of the Temple Area
\p
\v 15 The¦353104 man completed¦353101 measuring¦353104 the¦353105 temple¦353105 interior and then¦353101 took¦353107 me¦353107 out¦353107 to the eastern¦353113 gate¦353109 and¦353114 measured¦353114 all¦353115 the surrounding area there.
\v 16-19 He¦353119 measured¦353119 each side¦353120 \add of the wall\add* with¦353122 \add &his\add* measuring¦353123 rod, \add and it was\add* 270m\f + \fr 42:16 \ft Following the Septuagint here, which seems more accurate (unless he now has a shorter measuring rod).\f* long on¦353122 each of the eastern¦353121, northern, southern, and western sides¦353120.
\v 20 \add ≈So\add* the four¦353168 sides¦353169 that were measured¦353170 (and thus the wall¦353171 around¦353173 it¦353170) was¦353177 270m on each¦353173 side¦353169, and¦353179 the¦353179 wall separated¦353182 the¦353184 sacred¦353184 area from the \add ≈public area\add*.
\c 43
\s1 God's powerful splendour of God at the temple
\rem /s1 The Lord Returns to the Temple; The Divine Glory Returns to the Temple; The Lord's Glory Returns; The Glory Returns to the Temple
\p
\v 1 Then¦353187 the man took¦353187 me¦353187 to¦353188 the gate¦353191 that¦353192 opened to the east¦353195.
\v 2 Wow! The¦353198 splendour¦353198 of Yisrael's¦353200 god¦353199 \add ≈appeared towards\add* the east¦353203. His¦353210 voice¦353204 was¦353201 like¦353205 the¦353205 \add ≈roar of¦353205 rushing water¦353206\add*, and¦353197 the land¦353208 shone with his¦353210 \add ≈brilliance\add*.\x + \xo 43:2: \xt Eze 10:3-4,18-19; 11:22-23; Rev 1:15.\x*
\v 3 \add ≈What I saw¦353215 in this vision¦353213 was¦353216 like¦353216 what I'd seen¦353219 in my earlier\add* visions¦353225, first by the¦353231 Kebar¦353233 Canal, then¦353212 later when¦353220 he\f + \fr 43:3 \ft It's only one letter difference, but the Hebrew has ‘I’ here which seems wrong and is adjusted in some later manuscripts.\f* came¦353220 to¦353221 destroy¦353221 \add @Yerushalem¦353215\add*. I fell¦353234 to¦353235 \add my knees with\add* my face¦353237 \add to the ground\add*.
\v 4 \add ≈So¦353239\add* Yahweh's¦353240 \add ≈brightness¦353239\add* entered into¦353242 the¦353244 temple¦353244 via the eastern¦353250 entrance¦353246.
\p
\v 5 Then¦353252 the spirit¦353253 lifted¦353252 me¦353252 up¦353252 and¦353254 brought¦353254 me¦353254 into¦353255 the inner¦353258 court¦353257, and¦353259 wow, Yahweh's¦353263 brilliance was filling¦353260 the temple¦353264.
\v 6 Then¦353266 as the man¦353270 was¦353271 standing¦353272 \add there\add* beside¦353273 me, I heard¦353266 someone¦353267 speak¦353267 to¦353268 me¦353268 from¦353269 inside the temple¦353269,
\v 7 and¦353275 \add @the¦353276 voice¦353276\add* told¦353275 me¦353276, “Son¦353277 of¦353277 humanity¦353279, this is the place¦353311 \add where I'll sit on\add* my throne¦353283 and \add ≈rest\add* my¦353288 feet¦353288—where¦353289 I'll¦353290 live¦353290 in¦353308 the middle¦353293 of the¦353294 Israeli¦353294 people¦353294 forever¦353297. Yisrael's¦353296 \add ≈descendants¦353277\add* and their kings will¦353290 no longer profane my¦353305 sacred¦353305 name¦353304 with their faithlessness or with the¦353309 corpses¦353309 of their kings at¦353308 their \add ≈hilltop¦353311 shrines¦353311\add*.
\v 8 They'll no longer profane my¦353320 sacred name¦353328 by¦353330 putting¦353313 their¦353314 \add ≈entrance\add* next to my¦353317 \add ≈entrance\add*, and¦353318 their doorposts next to my doorposts, with nothing but¦353318 a wall¦353321 between¦353322 me¦353322 and¦353323 them¦353323. They¦353324 profaned my¦353329 sacred name with their¦353330 disgusting actions, so I¦353333 \add ≈destroyed¦353333\add* them¦353334 with my anger¦353335.
\v 9 Now¦353337 they're to remove their prostitution¦353341 and¦353342 \add ?their kings' corpses\add* from¦353344 in¦353346 front of¦353341 me¦353344, and¦353345 I'll¦353345 live¦353345 \add ≈among¦353346 them¦353346\add* forever¦353347.
\p
\v 10 “Son¦353351 of¦353351 humanity¦353353, \add ≈describe that¦353363 temple to the¦353357 Israeli¦353357 people¦353353\add* so¦353363 they'll¦353365 be¦353363 ashamed¦353363 of¦353364 their¦353364 \add ≈disobedience\add* and¦353365 \add ?≈think about¦353351 those plans\add*.
\v 11 If¦353370 they're¦353372 ashamed¦353372 of everything they've¦353376 done¦353376, then reveal the¦353377 design¦353377 of¦353377 that temple¦353378 to¦353398 them—its details, its entrances¦353381 and exits¦353380, its complete design, and all its decrees and rules. Then¦353370 \add right\add* \add ≈in front of them¦353399,\add* write¦353400 that down so¦353402 they'll¦353413 see its complete design and all its rules, and then obey them¦353414.
\v 12 This¦353416 is¦353417 the¦353417 regulation for the¦353418 temple: From the¦353421 peak¦353421 of¦353421 the¦353422 hill¦353422 to all¦353423 the surrounding border¦353425 around¦353426 it, it'll be¦353429 very sacred¦353429. Listen, that's the¦353434 regulation for the¦353435 temple.
\s1 The altar dimensions
\rem /s1 The Altar
\p
\v 13 These¦353437 are¦353438 the altar's¦353439 measurements¦353438, using the same kind of measuring rod that¦353456 was used for the temple area: There's a gutter¦353444 around the altar that's \add both\add* half a metre deep and¦353437 wide. There's a rim around¦353453 it that's 23cm wide. That'll be¦353457 the¦353457 base for \add the rest of\add* the¦353458 altar¦353458.\x + \xo 43:13-17: \xt Exo 27:1-2; 2Ch 4:1.\x*
\v 14 The¦353460 lower part of¦353460 the gutter¦353460 is one¦353470 metre high to the lower ledge that surrounds the altar. The lower ledge is half a metre wide. From¦353471 that ledge to the upper ledge, it's two¦353466 metres¦353467. That ledge is also¦353468 half a metre wide.
\v 15 The¦353482 altar¦353482 body \add at the top\add* is¦353483 another two metres¦353484 high and¦353482 has a \add ≈hornlike projection\add* jutting up \add from each of its corners\add*.
\v 16 The¦353492 sides¦353503 of¦353502 the¦353502 square¦353500 body are six metres long¦353496.
\v 17 The¦353505 upper ledge¦353505 that borders¦353515 the altar body is¦353518 also¦353515 a square, eight metres long¦353508 on¦353509 each¦353524 side, and¦353505 has a rim all¦353524 around¦353509 that's 27cm wide¦353511. There's a gutter¦353520 measuring half¦353518 a metre at¦353509 the bottom of the altar. There are¦353526 steps¦353525 leading up to¦353526 the east¦353527 side of the altar.”
\s1 The purification of the new altar
\rem /s1 The Consecration of the Altar
\p
\v 18 Then¦353529 \add @the¦353529 man¦353529\add* told¦353529 me¦353530, “Son¦353531 of¦353531 humanity¦353533, the master¦353536 Yahweh¦353536 says¦353535 that those¦353538 are¦353539 the dimensions for when they make¦353542 the altar¦353540 for presenting the burnt¦353545 offering¦353543 on¦353541, and¦353546 for sprinkling¦353546 blood¦353548 onto.\x + \xo 43:18-27: \xt Exo 29:35-37.\x*
\v 19 You must¦353550 give¦353550 a bull¦353565 from the¦353568 cattle as a sin¦353569 offering¦353569 for the Levitical¦353554 priests¦353553 who¦353555 are¦353557 Tsadok's¦353558 descendants¦353557—those who approach¦353559 me¦353560 to¦353564 serve¦353564 me. That's the master¦353562 Yahweh's¦353563 declaration¦353561.
\v 20 Then¦353571 you'll¦353571 take¦353571 some¦353572 of¦353572 \add @the¦353572 bull's¦353572\add* blood¦353572 and¦353573 place it on¦353574 the¦353576 altar's ‘horns¦353577’ and¦353578 on the¦353580 four¦353580 top edges and¦353583 the¦353585 surrounding¦353586 rim. You'll purify¦353587 it¦353588 and¦353589 \add ≈make¦353589 it¦353589 allowable in my presence\add*.
\v 21 Then¦353591 take¦353591 that sin¦353594 offering¦353594 bull¦353593 and¦353595 burn¦353595 it¦353595 in¦353596 the¦353596 designated part of¦353596 the¦353597 temple¦353597 area outside¦353598 the sanctuary¦353599.
\v 22 Then¦353601 on¦353601 the¦353602 second¦353602 day¦353601, you'll¦353603 get from¦353609 the goats¦353606 a male¦353604 goat¦353604 without any blemishes, and¦353601 offer¦353608 it as a sin¦353614 offering¦353608—the priests will¦353603 purify¦353609 the¦353612 altar¦353612 just¦353613 as they¦353614 purified¦353614 it with¦353601 the bull¦353615.
\v 23 When¦353617 you¦353617 finish¦353617 that purification¦353618, offer an unblemished bull¦353620 from the¦353623 cattle and¦353625 an unblemished ram¦353625 from¦353626 the¦353628 flock¦353628,
\v 24 and offer¦353638 them to \add me,\add* Yahweh. The¦353632 priests¦353635 will¦353634 throw¦353634 salt¦353637 onto them and present¦353631 them as a burnt¦353640 offering¦353640 to¦353641 Yahweh.
\v 25 Then every day¦353649 for \add the next\add* seven¦353643 days¦353644 you¦353645 must bring a billy goat¦353646 to the priest to be sacrificed for an offering¦353648, so I'll accept the altar. You¦353645 must also¦353650 sacrifice¦353648 a young¦353651 bull¦353650 and¦353654 a ram¦353654, each without any defect, that the¦353657 priests will¦353659 provide.
\v 26 They¦353663 must atone¦353663 for¦353663 the¦353666 altar¦353666 for seven¦353661 days¦353662 and¦353667 purify¦353667 it, and in that way they¦353663 must consecrate it¦353668 \add to me\add*.
\v 27 \add ≈After doing all that\add*, from the eighth¦353680 day¦353679 onward¦353681 the¦353683 priests¦353683 will¦353682 prepare you \add people\add*'s burnt¦353689 offerings and your peace¦353692 offerings on¦353679 the altar¦353686, and I'll¦353693 accept¦353693 you¦353694. That's the¦353695 master¦353696 Yahweh's¦353697 declaration¦353695.”
\c 44
\s1 The locked gate
\rem /s1 The Prince, the Levites, the Priests; The Use of the East Gate; The Prince, Levites, and Priests; The Closed Gate
\p
\v 1 Then the man took¦353700 me¦353701 back¦353700 to the outer¦353705 sanctuary¦353704 gate¦353703 facing¦353706 east¦353707, \add ≈but¦353700\add* it was¦353709 shut¦353709,
\v 2 and¦353711 Yahweh told¦353711 me¦353712, “This¦353715 gate¦353714 has¦353730 been sealed shut—it won't¦353718 be¦353717 opened¦353719. No person will¦353719 go¦353723 through it¦353724, because¦353725 Yisrael's¦353729 god¦353727 Yahweh entered through it¦353731, so it's¦353732 \add now\add* closed.
\v 3 \add Only Yisrael's\add* ruler will¦353740 sit¦353740 by it¦353742 to¦353743 eat¦353743 food¦353745 \add ≈in¦353748 front¦353748 of¦353748\add* Yahweh¦353749, \add but\add* he'll¦353740 enter via the gate's¦353752 porch¦353751, and¦353754 leave¦353755 the same way¦353754.”
\s1 Foreigners disallowed in the temple
\rem /s1 Rules for Admission to the Temple; Admission to the Temple
\p
\v 4 Then¦353757 he¦353757 took¦353757 me¦353757 to¦353762 the¦353764 front¦353764 of the temple by way¦353758 of the northern¦353761 gate¦353760, and¦353766 I looked¦353766, and¦353767 wow, Yahweh's¦353771 brilliance filled¦353768 \add *his¦353769\add* temple, and¦353776 I fell¦353776 to \add my knees with\add* my face¦353764 \add bowed towards the ground\add*.
\v 5 Then¦353781 Yahweh¦353805 told¦353781 me¦353782, “Son¦353784 of¦353784 humanity¦353786, \add ≈pay attention\add* and¦353789 look¦353789 with¦353790 your eyes¦353790 and¦353791 listen¦353792 with your ears¦353791 to¦353800 everything¦353800 that¦353796 I'm¦353797 declaring to¦353806 you¦353810: all the instructions¦353802 about¦353784 Yahweh's¦353783 temple and¦353806 all¦353806 its¦353808 regulations¦353802. \add ≈Take special note of\add* the temple's entrance¦353812 and exits¦353815.
\v 6 Then¦353818 tell¦353818 the rebellious¦353821 \add ≈Israelis¦353824\add* that the master¦353828 Yahweh¦353828 says¦353827 this: Let all¦353833 your¦353835 disgusting actions be enough¦353830 for¦353832 you¦353832 Israeli¦353824 people—
\v 7 that you brought¦353839 foreigners¦353842 with uncircumcised¦353843 hearts¦353845 and¦353846 uncircumcised¦353846 flesh¦353847 to¦353848 be¦353848 in¦353849 my sanctuary¦353849, profaning¦353850 my temple¦353853, while you \add people\add* were¦353848 offering¦353854 me food¦353857, fat¦353858, and¦353859 blood¦353859. You¦353854 \add people\add* have broken¦353860 my agreement¦353863 with all¦353865 your disgusting actions.
\v 8 You haven't carried out your duties regarding my¦353872 sacred things¦353872, but¦353869 \add instead,\add* you \add people\add* appointed¦353873 others to take up your duties, and¦353873 you assigned¦353873 them¦353873 to¦353874 care for¦353877 my sacred place.
\p
\v 9 “The¦353904 master¦353882 Yahweh¦353882 says¦353881 this: No¦353883 foreigner¦353888, from¦353898 any¦353898 of¦353900 those who¦353903 live among the¦353905 Israeli¦353906 people¦353905, may enter my sacred place if they're not¦353893 physically¦353892 circumcised or if they \add ≈haven't declared themself to¦353898 be one of¦353905 my people\add*.
\s1 Demotion of the Levites
\rem /s1 The Levites Are Excluded from the Priesthood
\p
\v 10 The¦353916 Levites¦353911 went¦353915 far from me—wandering away from me \add and\add* going after¦353920 their¦353923 idols¦353921, but¦353922 they'll¦353922 be punished¦353923.
\v 11 They're \add still\add* servants in¦353926 my¦353926 sanctuary¦353926, watching the¦353931 temple gates¦353931 and serving¦353927 inside, and¦353933 they slaughter¦353938 the¦353941 burnt¦353941 offerings¦353941 and¦353942 the¦353944 people's¦353945 sacrifices¦353944, and they'll¦353925 stand¦353947 in front¦353948 of¦353926 the people¦353945 and¦353946 serve¦353949 them¦353948.
\v 12 \add However,\add* because¦353951 they¦353953 sacrificed to¦353955 their idols¦353956, they¦353957 became sinful stumbling¦353961 blocks¦353961 for the \add ≈Israeli¦353958 people\add*, therefore¦353963 I've \add ≈promised to take action\add* against them¦353968, and¦353972 they'll¦353957 \add ≈pay for their disobedience\add*. That's¦353952 the master¦353970 Yahweh's¦353971 declaration¦353969.
\v 13 They¦353977 mustn't come near¦353977 to¦353978 me¦353978 to¦353979 act as¦353979 my priests¦353979 or to approach any of¦353984 my sacred things, the most sacred things. Instead, they'll¦353977 bear¦353991 their shame¦353992 and¦353975 their guilt for the disgusting things they've¦353995 done¦353995,
\v 14 but¦353997 I'll¦353997 place them¦353998 as housekeepers¦354001 in the temple¦354001—for all¦354002 its¦354003 \add mundane\add* duties and¦353997 \add ≈all the menial work¦354003\add* that's¦354005 done¦354006 in¦354007 it¦354007.
\s1 Tsadok's descendants as priests
\rem /s1 The Levitical Priests; The Priests
\p
\v 15 Then the Levitical¦354011 priests¦354010, those descendants¦354012 of¦354012 Tsadok¦354013 who¦354014 fulfilled the¦354018 duties of my¦354030 sanctuary¦354019 when¦354020 the¦354021 Israeli¦354021 people¦354021 were wandering away from¦354024 me¦354024, they'll¦354026 approach¦354026 me¦354027 to¦354028 worship¦354028 me¦354028. They'll stand¦354029 in¦354020 front¦354030 of me to¦354031 offer¦354031 me¦354032 the fat¦354033 and¦354034 the blood¦354034. That's the¦354035 master¦354036 Yahweh's¦354037 declaration¦354035.
\v 16 They'll come¦354040 to¦354041 my sanctuary¦354043 and approach¦354040 my¦354043 table¦354048 to¦354049 worship¦354049 me¦354049 and¦354050 to fulfill their duties to me.
\v 17 When¦354055 they come¦354056 to¦354057 the¦354059 gates¦354059 of¦354059 the inner¦354061 courtyard, they'll¦354064 have to dress in linen¦354063 clothing¦354062, because they¦354064 mustn't \add ≈wear\add* wool¦354069 inside¦354074 the courtyard gates or in the temple.\x + \xo 44:17-18: \xt Exo 28:39-43; Lev 16:4.\x*
\v 18 \add ≈They¦354078 must¦354078 wear\add* linen turbans¦354076 on¦354090 their heads¦354081 and¦354082 linen underclothes¦354082 around their waists¦354087. They¦354089 mustn't dress in¦354090 clothing that makes them sweat¦354090.
\v 19 When¦354092 they¦354092 go¦354092 \add back\add* out¦354092 to¦354093 the outer¦354096 courtyard¦354095 to¦354097 go \add back again\add* to¦354101 the¦354103 people¦354103, they¦354104 must take off¦354104 the clothes¦354118 they wore when¦354092 they¦354110 served¦354111—they must take them off and¦354092 lay them¦354112 down¦354113 in¦354092 a sacred room¦354115, so they¦354117 don't¦354120 make other¦354119 people¦354125 sacred by¦354126 contact with¦354092 their¦354126 special clothing.\x + \xo 44:19: \xt Lev 16:23.\x*
\p
\v 20 \add @The¦354128 priests¦354128\add* mustn't shave¦354130 their heads or allow¦354133 their hair to hang loosely, but¦354128 they¦354133 must¦354130 \add ≈keep¦354128 the¦354128 hair on¦354128 their heads¦354128 neatly¦354128 trimmed¦354128\add*.
\v 21 No priest¦354146 should drink¦354143 wine¦354140 \add ≈before\add* he comes¦354147 to¦354148 the¦354150 inner¦354151 court¦354150.\x + \xo 44:21: \xt Lev 10:9.\x*
\v 22 \add ≈Also¦354166, priests can't¦354155 marry widows of men who weren't priests, or women who've been divorced¦354154—they're permitted to¦354158 marry only Israeli¦354165 women who've never been¦354169 married, or widows¦354170 of¦354171 other priests¦354171\add*.
\p
\v 23 They¦354177 must¦354177 teach the people¦354176 the difference between¦354178 things that are sacred¦354179 and¦354180 things¦354180 that are not, and teach them¦354185 how to know¦354185 what things are acceptable to¦354185 me and what things are not.\x + \xo 44:23: \xt Lev 10:10.\x*
\v 24 In¦354204 a dispute¦354189 they¦354191 must judge¦354192 in¦354194 accordance with¦354194 my instructions¦354203 and regulations¦354203. They¦354190 must keep¦354207 my law¦354200 and my statutes¦354203 at¦354194 every religious celebration, and they¦354207 must observe¦354207 my¦354210 sacred rest days.
\p
\v 25 \add @Priests¦354218\add* mustn't defile themselves by going¦354218 near¦354213 a person's¦354216 dead body. However, if¦354220 the dead person¦354216 was his father¦354223 or mother¦354224, son¦354225 or daughter¦354226, or brother¦354227 or unmarried sister¦354228, then¦354213 they¦354235 can defile themselves¦354235,
\v 26 and¦354237 after¦354237 they've been purified¦354238, they must¦354241 \add ≈wait\add* for seven¦354239 days¦354240.
\v 27 Then¦354245 \add after that\add*, \add ≈when\add* he goes¦354246 into¦354247 the inner¦354253 courtyard¦354252 to¦354254 serve¦354254 in¦354245 the sacred¦354249 place¦354255, he¦354256 must¦354256 bring¦354256 a sin¦354257 offering¦354257 for himself. That's the¦354258 master¦354259 Yahweh's¦354260 declaration¦354258.
\p
\v 28 \add ≈The priests must¦354262 not¦354268 own¦354267 any land\add*—I¦354265 am¦354274 their inheritance¦354264 and¦354262 I'll be¦354266 their property.\x + \xo 44:28: \xt Num 18:20.\x*
\v 29 They'll have the¦354276 food offerings¦354276, the¦354277 sin¦354277 offerings¦354277, and¦354278 the¦354278 guilt¦354278 offerings¦354278 to eat¦354280, plus¦354277 everything in¦354284 Yisrael¦354284 that's devoted¦354283 to¦354285 Yahweh will¦354286 be theirs.\x + \xo 44:29-30: \xt Num 18:8-19.\x*
\v 30 The¦354288 best¦354288 of the¦354291 firstfruits¦354291 \add ≈from every¦354292 harvest\add* and¦354293 every¦354293 contribution¦354295 \add from the people\add* will¦354300 belong¦354300 to the priests, and you \add Israelis\add* will¦354303 give¦354303 the first¦354301 of¦354301 your \add bread\add* dough¦354302\f + \fr 44:30 \ft Probably after it's cooked???\f* to¦354304 the priests¦354304 so that blessing¦354306 may rest¦354305 on your home¦354309.
\v 31 Priests¦354323 must¦354322 not¦354321 eat¦354322 \add ≈the¦354317 meat of¦354311 any bird¦354317 or animal that's found dead or that's been killed by any other\add* animal.\x + \xo 44:31: \xt Lev 22:8.\x*
\c 45
\s1 Yahweh's section of land
\rem /s1 Division of the Land; The Holy District; The Lord's Portion of the Country
\p
\v 1 When¦354326 you¦354326 throw dice to divide up¦354331 the land as an inheritance¦354330 \add for the tribes\add*, you must¦354331 make¦354326 an¦354330 offering¦354331 of land to¦354333 Yahweh¦354333. That offering will become a¦354335 sacred part¦354335 of the¦354338 land¦354338: \add ≈thirteen kilometres by ten¦354345 kilometres in¦354326 size\add*.\f + \fr 45:1 \ft The ‘ten kilometres’ (twenty thousand cubits) from the Septuagint appears to correct the Hebrew which has half of that, but then v3 and v4 wouldn't make sense.\f* \add ≈That entire¦354350 area\add* will become sacred.
\v 2 \add ≈Within that¦354356 area,\add* a 270m wide square¦354364 area that'll surround the¦354359 sacred¦354359 place¦354359 \add ≈will¦354355 be¦354355 left empty\add*, with¦354362 a further border around¦354365 it¦354369 twenty-seven metres wide.
\v 3 Inside that¦354375 \add first, larger\add* area¦354374, measure¦354376 a¦354377 \add rectangle\add* thirteen kilometres by five¦354378 kilometres for the¦354388 sanctuary¦354388—the most sacred¦354389 place¦354389.
\v 4 It'll be¦354392 a sacred place in the¦354395 nation¦354395 for the priests¦354397 who¦354398 serve¦354398 Yahweh, who come near Yahweh to¦354402 serve¦354402 him. It'll be¦354406 a place¦354408 for¦354409 their houses¦354409 and¦354410 a sacred area for the sacred place.
\v 5 The¦354424 \add other half\add*, which is \add also\add* thirteen kilometres long¦354416 and¦354413 five¦354413 kilometres wide¦354419, will be for towns for the Levites¦354422 who¦354423 serve¦354423 in the temple¦354424.
\p
\v 6 You'll designate an area thirteen kilometres long¦354436 by half a kilometre wide¦354435 for the city that will be next to¦354440 the¦354441 area reserved for the¦354442 holy place. This city will¦354447 belong¦354447 to all¦354443 the¦354445 \add ≈Israeli¦354445 people\add*.
\s1 The leader's section of land
\rem /s1 Land for the Prince
\p
\v 7 The¦354449 leader's land will be on both sides of¦354453 the area reserved for the holy place and¦354462 the city¦354466—on the west¦354469 and¦354471 the¦354471 east. Its length¦354475 will \add ≈be the¦354478 same as those portions¦354478 that¦354450 it's parallel¦354476 to¦354481\add*.
\v 8 That¦354504 area will¦354487 belong¦354487 to¦354490 Yisrael's¦354491 leader \add so\add* the leaders¦354496 won't¦354492 keep oppressing¦354494 my¦354496 people¦354499, \add ≈but¦354492 rather\add* they'll¦354494 give¦354501 the rest of¦354499 the land¦354486 to¦354502 \add ≈be divided among the¦354502 tribes¦354505\add*.
\rem /s1 Rules for the Prince
\v 9 The¦354528 master¦354529 Yahweh¦354530 says¦354510 this: \add ≈That's plenty\add* for¦354515 you, leaders¦354516 of¦354516 Yisrael¦354517! \add ≈Replace¦354517 your\add* violence¦354519 and¦354521 strife \add ≈with\add* justice¦354521 and¦354522 righteousness¦354522. Quit your evictions¦354525 of my¦354527 people¦354527. That's the¦354528 master Yahweh's¦354512 declaration¦354528.
\s1 Dealing with produce and animals
\rem /s1 Weights and Measures
\p
\v 10 You \add Israelis\add* must \add ≈use accurate scales and¦354535 measuring containers\add*.\x + \xo 45:10: \xt Lev 19:36.\x*
\v 11 \add ≈The¦354545 baskets for measuring¦354546 dry things and containers¦354549 for measuring liquids (called an ‘efah’ for dry things and a ‘bath¦354545’ for liquids, both a tenth¦354553 of¦354553 the¦354554 large 220 litre ‘homer¦354554’) must be¦354559 the same volume—each must hold twenty-two litres\add*.
\v 12 \add ≈For¦354575 weights, the¦354562 shekel¦354562 weight must¦354563 be¦354563 ten¦354570 grams. (Twenty¦354563 ‘gerahs¦354564’ makes one shekel, then¦354562 sixty shekels¦354569 makes one ‘mina¦354573’.)\add*
\rem /s1 Special Offerings and Celebrations; Offerings; Offerings and Holy Days
\p
\v 13 You \add Israelis\add* must¦354578 present¦354585 \add to the leader\add* \add ≈one measure of¦354581 wheat¦354584 or barley¦354588 for every sixty measures\add* \add that you harvest\add*,
\v 14 \add ≈one measure of¦354590 olive¦354593 oil¦354591 for¦354602 every one hundred measures\add* \add that you produce\add*,
\v 15 plus¦354620 one¦354610 sheep¦354608 or goat from¦354611 the¦354613 flock¦354613 for every two¦354616 hundred¦354616 animals from¦354617 the¦354617 watered regions¦354617 of¦354617 Yisrael¦354618, to be used for any burnt¦354620 offering¦354620 or peace¦354621 offering¦354621 to¦354622 make¦354622 atonement¦354622 for the people. That's the master¦354625 Yahweh's¦354626 declaration¦354624.
\p
\v 16 All¦354628 the¦354629 people¦354629 in¦354637 the country¦354630 will¦354631 give that¦354635 contribution¦354634 to¦354632 Yisrael's¦354637 leader¦354636.
\v 17 It'll be¦354642 the leader's responsibility to supply the animals for the burnt¦354643 offerings¦354643, the¦354644 grain¦354644 offerings¦354644, and¦354645 the¦354645 drink¦354645 offerings¦354645 at¦354646 the festivals¦354646 and¦354647 the new moon¦354647 celebrations, and on¦354646 the rest days—all the \add ≈Israeli¦354653 people's\add* scheduled festivals. He'll¦354656 provide¦354656 for the¦354659 sin¦354659 offerings¦354659, the¦354662 grain¦354662 offerings¦354662, the¦354665 burnt¦354665 offerings¦354665, and¦354666 the¦354668 peace¦354668 offerings¦354668 \add ≈to¦354669 make¦354669 the¦354671 Israeli¦354673 people right\add* \add with God\add*.
\s1 The festivals
\rem /s1 Festivals; The Festivals
\r (Exo. 12:1-20; Lev. 23:33-43)
\p
\v 18 The¦354680 master¦354679 Yahweh¦354679 says¦354678 this: On¦354682 the first \add day\add* of¦354683 the first month¦354683 \add of the year\add*, you¦354684 must¦354684 take¦354684 an unblemished¦354690 bull¦354685 from¦354691 the herd¦354689 and¦354691 perform a sin¦354691 offering \add to purify\add* the¦354694 temple.
\v 19 The¦354697 priest¦354697 will¦354696 take¦354696 some¦354698 of¦354698 the¦354698 blood¦354698 of¦354698 that sin¦354699 offering¦354699 and¦354700 place it¦354700 on the¦354703 doorposts¦354703 of¦354703 the¦354704 temple¦354704 and¦354705 on the¦354707 four¦354707 corners¦354708 of¦354708 the¦354709 border of¦354710 the altar¦354710, and¦354711 on¦354711 the¦354713 doorposts¦354713 of¦354713 the¦354714 gate¦354714 to¦354701 the inner¦354716 court¦354715.
\v 20 You'll do¦354719 this \add again\add* on¦354720 the seventh¦354720 \add day\add* of that month¦354721 for¦354725 each person's¦354724 accidental sin, or sin by ignorance¦354724. \add In that way you'll\add* \add ≈make¦354725 the¦354728 temple¦354728 pure\add* \add in God's sight\add*.
\p
\v 21 Then on¦354731 the¦354737 fourteenth day of¦354734 that first¦354730 month¦354734, you \add Israelis\add* will¦354735 have a seven-day celebration, \add ≈when you'll eat¦354742 bread¦354741 made without any raising agent\add*.\x + \xo 45:21: \xt Exo 12:1-20; Num 28:16-25.\x*
\v 22 That¦354747 same day¦354746, the leader¦354745 will¦354744 prepare a bull¦354754 as a sin¦354755 offering¦354755 for himself¦354748 and¦354744 for all¦354750 the¦354752 people¦354752 in¦354746 the country¦354753.
\v 23 For¦354763 the seven days of¦354758 the celebration, the leader will¦354761 prepare a burnt¦354762 offering¦354762 for Yahweh¦354763: Seven¦354764 bulls¦354765 and¦354766 seven¦354766 unblemished¦354768 rams¦354767 each day¦354769 for seven¦354770 days¦354771, and¦354772 a male¦354773 goat¦354773 each day as a sin¦354772 offering¦354772.
\v 24 Then¦354777 the leader will¦354782 perform a food offering¦354777 of twenty-two litres \add of flour\add* with seven litres of \add olive\add* oil¦354783 for¦354779 each bull¦354779 and¦354777 each ram¦354781.
\p
\v 25 On¦354788 the fifteenth day¦354790 of¦354791 the¦354795 seventh¦354795 month¦354791 \add of the year\add*, at¦354788 the \add ‘temporary shelters’\add* celebration, the leader will¦354793 perform offerings on¦354788 those¦354794 seven¦354795 days¦354796: Sin offerings¦354797, burnt¦354798 offerings¦354798, food offerings¦354799, and¦354800 offerings of oil¦354800.\x + \xo 45:25: \xt Lev 23:33-36; Num 29:12-38.\x*
\c 46
\s1 The leader and the festivals
\rem /s1 Miscellaneous Regulations; The Prince and the Festivals
\p
\v 1 The master¦354806 Yahweh¦354806 says¦354805 this: The eastern¦354812 gate¦354808 of¦354808 the inner¦354810 courtyard¦354809 will¦354813 be¦354813 shut¦354814 for the¦354815 six¦354815 days¦354816 of¦354816 work¦354817, but¦354818 it'll¦354813 be¦354820 opened¦354820 on rest days and¦354821 on¦354821 the¦354821 day¦354821 of¦354821 the¦354822 new¦354822 moon¦354822.
\v 2 The¦354826 leader¦354826 will¦354825 enter¦354825 the outer courtyard by the path¦354827 to that gate¦354829 and its outside¦354830 porch¦354828, and¦354831 he'll stand¦354831 at¦354832 the¦354834 doorposts¦354834 of¦354834 the¦354835 inner gate¦354835 while the¦354837 priests¦354837 perform his¦354840 burnt¦354840 offering and¦354841 peace¦354843 offering. Then¦354825 he'll worship¦354844 \add ≈right there\add* at the inner gate and \add ≈then¦354825 leave again\add*, but¦354825 the gate won't¦354851 be¦354853 shut¦354853 until¦354854 evening¦354856.
\v 3 The¦354859 people¦354859 will¦354858 also¦354866 worship¦354858 in¦354865 front¦354867 of¦354862 Yahweh¦354868 at¦354865 the¦354859 entrance¦354862 to¦354867 that¦354864 gate¦354863 on¦354865 rest days and¦354858 new moons¦354866.
\v 4 The¦354870 burnt¦354870 offering¦354870 that¦354871 the¦354874 leader¦354874 offers¦354870 to¦354875 Yahweh¦354875 on¦354876 the rest day¦354876 will¦354878 be¦354878 six¦354878 unblemished lambs¦354879 and¦354881 an unblemished ram¦354881.
\v 5 The¦354889 grain¦354884 offering¦354884 that he gives with the ram¦354886 is twenty-two litres \add of flour\add*, and¦354884 with the lambs¦354887 it should be¦354885 as much as he pleases, along with one litre of \add olive\add* oil¦354891 for¦354886 every twenty-two litres of flour.
\v 6 On¦354895 the¦354895 day¦354895 of¦354895 the¦354896 new¦354896 moon¦354896 he must¦354906 offer a bull¦354897, six¦354902 lambs¦354903, and¦354895 a ram¦354904—\add ≈all without any defects\add*.
\v 7 He¦354912 must¦354912 \add also\add* provide¦354912 a grain¦354913 offering¦354913 of twenty-two litres of flour with the bull¦354909, the same with the ram¦354911, and¦354908 as much as he¦354912 wants with the lambs¦354914, along with a litre of \add olive\add* oil¦354918 with every twenty-two litres of flour.
\v 8 The¦354923 leader¦354923 enters¦354922 by the path to the gate¦354926 and its¦354928 porch¦354925, and he¦354929 must¦354927 leave¦354929 by the same path.
\p
\v 9 But¦354931 when¦354931 the land's¦354934 people¦354932 come¦354960 in¦354931 front¦354935 of¦354932 Yahweh¦354936 at¦354931 the scheduled¦354937 festivals, anyone entering through the north gate to¦354943 worship¦354943 must leave through the south gate, and¦354931 anyone entering through the south gate must leave through the north gate. No one¦354938 is allowed to¦354958 turn¦354960 back to the gate¦354962 through which¦354963 they entered¦354965, because¦354967 everyone¦354932 must keep going straight ahead until they get out¦354969.
\v 10 The¦354972 leader¦354972 must be¦354973 amongst¦354973 them¦354973 in their¦354973 midst¦354973—when they go in, he must go in, and¦354972 when¦354974 they¦354974 leave, he¦354975 must leave.
\v 11 During the festivals¦354979 \add that you hold for me\add*, the king must¦354981 present twenty-two litres of flour along with a bull¦354984 or a ram¦354986, and¦354979 as much flour as he wants to¦354992 with the lambs¦354987, along with a litre of¦354988 \add olive\add* oil¦354990 with every twenty-two litres of flour.
\p
\v 12 When¦354995 the¦354998 leader¦354998 gives a voluntary offering to¦355005 Yahweh¦355005 (either a burnt offering or a peace offering), the¦355010 eastern¦355012 gate will be opened¦355006 for¦355005 him¦355007. He'll¦354997 offer¦355013 his¦355016 burnt¦355016 offering¦355019 or his¦355019 peace¦355019 offering just¦355020 like¦355020 he¦355021 does on¦355022 the rest day¦355022. Then he must¦354997 go¦355024 out¦355024, and¦355025 after¦355029 he's¦355030 gone¦355030 out, the gate will be shut¦355025.
\s1 The daily sacrifice
\rem /s1 The Daily Offering
\p
\v 13 In¦355041 addition¦355032, \add ≈every morning¦355041 someone\add*\f + \fr 46:13 \ft Some of the earliest translations (the Septuagint and the Vulgate) have ‘he’ here, suggesting it must be the leader.\f* \add ≈must¦355037 provide\add* a one year¦355035 old lamb¦355032 with¦355041 no¦355036 defects¦355036 to be \add ≈completely burnt¦355038 up as an\add* offering¦355038 to Yahweh¦355040.
\v 14 \add Also\add* \add ≈every morning¦355049\add* someone must¦355047 provide an offering¦355047 of four litres of¦355054 flour¦355059 mixed with¦355049 a litre of \add olive\add* oil¦355053, \add and\add* \add ≈that's a permanent¦355063 requirement\add*.
\v 15 They'll prepare the¦355070 lamb¦355070, the¦355073 grain¦355073 offering, and¦355066 the¦355076 oil¦355076, \add ≈every morning\add*—a permanent \add daily\add* burnt¦355079 offering¦355079.
\s1 The leader and the country
\rem /s1 The Prince and the Land
\p
\v 16 The¦355087 master¦355086 Yahweh¦355086 says¦355085 this: If¦355088 the¦355091 leader¦355091 gives¦355090 a gift¦355092 to¦355093 any of¦355095 his sons, it becomes his¦355097 inheritance—that is, it'll¦355098 become his sons' property¦355099 \add because\add* it's¦355098 an¦355101 inheritance¦355101.
\v 17 \add ≈But¦355103\add* if he¦355105 gives¦355105 a gift¦355106 from¦355107 his inheritance to¦355108 one¦355108 of¦355109 his servants¦355109, then it'll belong¦355122 to that servant¦355109 until¦355112 the¦355114 year¦355114 of¦355114 liberty¦355115, and¦355103 then¦355103 it'll return¦355116 to¦355117 the leader¦355117. His¦355119 inheritance must¦355105 definitely be for¦355121 his \add own\add* sons¦355120.\x + \xo 46:17: \xt Lev 25:10.\x*
\v 18 The¦355127 leader¦355127 mustn't take¦355126 the¦355128 people's¦355129 inheritance¦355128 away¦355126 from them, evicting them from their own property—he must¦355126 provide for his¦355132 sons¦355136 from his¦355136 own property so¦355137 that¦355137 my¦355142 people won't¦355124 be¦355141 \add ≈forced off their own inherited¦355133 land\add*.”
\s1 The temple kitchens
\rem /s1 The Temple Kitchens
\p
\v 19 Next¦355162 the man brought¦355146 me¦355146 through the entrance¦355147 at¦355147 the gate¦355152 to¦355153 the sacred¦355156 rooms¦355155 for the priests¦355159 which¦355148 were¦355149 north-facing¦355161, and¦355162 what's more, there¦355164 was¦355164 a place¦355165 toward¦355168 the west¦355168.
\v 20 He¦355171 told¦355171 me¦355172, “That's the¦355174 place¦355174 where¦355175 the¦355179 priests¦355179 must boil¦355176 the¦355182 guilt¦355182 offering¦355182 and¦355183 the¦355185 sin¦355185 offering¦355185 and where¦355186 they¦355187 must bake¦355187 the¦355190 grain¦355190 offering. They¦355187 mustn't bring¦355192 the offerings¦355190 into¦355193 the outer¦355196 courtyard¦355195, because then the people¦355200 would become sacred¦355197 \add (and not be able to leave)\add*.”
\p
\v 21 Then¦355202 he¦355202 brought¦355202 me¦355202 to¦355208 the outer¦355206 courtyard and¦355207 led me¦355207 past the¦355210 four¦355210 corners¦355211 of¦355211 that courtyard¦355212, and¦355213 I¦355213 saw¦355213 that in¦355215 every corner¦355215 of¦355215 the¦355216 courtyard there was¦355218 \add another\add* \add ≈enclosed area\add*,
\v 22 and each was¦355226 twenty-two metres long¦355227 and¦355228 sixteen metres wide¦355229.
\v 23 Around¦355237 the inside of each¦355237 of¦355240 those¦355240 enclosed areas was¦355242 a stone¦355244 ledge, with cooking hearths¦355241 \add all around\add* under¦355243 the¦355244 ledge.
\v 24 The¦355250 man told¦355247 me¦355248, “Those¦355251 are¦355250 the places where¦355252 the temple workers will¦355253 boil¦355253 the people's¦355261 sacrifices¦355260.”
\c 47
\s1 The healing river
\rem /s1 The Stream Flowing from the Temple; The River From the Temple; The River of Healing; Water Flowing from the Temple
\p
\v 1 Then¦355263 the man took¦355263 me¦355263 back¦355263 to¦355264 the¦355266 temple entrance¦355266, and¦355268 there was water flowing out¦355271 from¦355272 under¦355272 the \add ≈front¦355278 edge of the building\add* toward¦355275 the east¦355280, and¦355281 the¦355281 water¦355281 was¦355282 flowing down¦355282 the south¦355286 side¦355284 of¦355284 the temple, to the right of the altar¦355288.\x + \xo 47:1: \xt Zech 14:8; Yhn 7:38; Rev 22:1.\x*
\v 2 So¦355290 he¦355290 took me¦355290 out¦355290 through the northern¦355294 gate and¦355295 led me¦355295 around¦355295 \add the outside\add* to¦355298 the eastern¦355304 gate, and¦355305 look, the water¦355307 was¦355308 flowing from¦355309 this gate on its south¦355312 side¦355311.
\v 3 As the¦355316 man¦355316 \add ≈started walking\add* toward the east¦355317 holding his measuring¦355318 line in¦355322 his hand¦355319, he¦355320 measured¦355320 off five hundred metres¦355322 and¦355323 led me¦355323 through¦355322 the water¦355324, which was ankle-deep¦355326.
\v 4 Then¦355328 he¦355328 measured¦355328 another five hundred metres and¦355330 led me¦355330 through¦355336 the water¦355331, which was \add now\add* knee-deep, and then¦355328 after another five hundred metres it was \add ≈up to the waist¦355338\add*.
\v 5 Finally¦355346 he¦355340 measured¦355340 off another five hundred metres, but¦355340 the¦355351 water had¦355350 become deep enough to swim¦355353 in and¦355340 too deep to¦355347 cross¦355347—it¦355358 was¦355358 \add now\add* a river¦355342 that¦355343 couldn't be¦355346 walked¦355358 through¦355358.
\v 6 The¦355369 man¦355365 \add ≈asked¦355360\add* me¦355361, “Son¦355363 of¦355363 humanity¦355365, \add ≈have you¦355362 taken¦355367 note of all\add* this?”
\p Then¦355360 he¦355366 led¦355366 me out and¦355360 had me \add ≈return\add* along the¦355368 riverbank¦355369.
\v 7 As I walked back, wow, \add now\add* the¦355375 riverbank¦355376 had many¦355378 trees¦355377 on each side.
\v 8 The¦355385 man told¦355383 me¦355384, “This water¦355385 \add ≈flows eastward and¦355392 down¦355392 into¦355393 the¦355395 Rift Valley\add*. \add *It'll\add* flow into¦355397 the¦355397 Dead Sea¦355397 and¦355402 will¦355402 make it¦355383 into fresh¦355402 water¦355404.
\v 9 \add ≈As a result,\add* every¦355407 kind of¦355407 living¦355410 creature¦355409 that¦355412 swarms¦355414 will live where¦355418 that water¦355430 flows. There'll be¦355432 lots of fish¦355424 when that water flows \add @into¦355428 the¦355428 Dead¦355428 Sea¦355428\add* as it¦355423 causes the salty water to¦355415 become¦355432 fresh¦355432 water—everything¦355434 will¦355433 live¦355433 wherever¦355435 that river¦355439 goes.
\v 10 Then¦355441 the fishermen¦355445 from¦355446 Eyn¦355446-Gedi¦355447 will¦355454 stand¦355442 \add there\add* by the water, and¦355441 there'll be¦355441 a place¦355452 to¦355455 dry out the fishing nets¦355453 near Eyn-Eglaim. There'll be many¦355461 kinds¦355455 of¦355457 fish in the Dead Sea—plentiful just like¦355458 the¦355458 fish¦355458 in the¦355459 Mediterranean Sea¦355459.
\v 11 \add However,\add* the Dead Sea's swamps¦355464 and¦355466 marshes¦355466 won't¦355467 be made fresh—they'll¦355468 be for providing salt¦355469.
\v 12 Beside that river¦355474 on¦355476 both banks¦355478, all kinds of¦355478 fruit trees¦355485 will grow¦355475. Their leaves will¦355490 never wilt, and¦355492 \add ≈they'll never fail to fruit\add*—\add in fact\add* \add *they'll\add* bear¦355497 fruit every¦355483 month¦355496 because¦355498 that water¦355499 from¦355500 the¦355502 temple flows to them. Their fruit will¦355505 be¦355505 \add ≈good to eat\add*, and their¦355499 leaves will¦355510 \add ≈help with\add* healing¦355510.\x + \xo 47:12: \xt Rev 22:2.\x*
\s1 Land boundaries
\rem /s1 Division of the land among the tribes; Boundaries for the Land; The New Boundaries of the Land; The Boundaries of the Land
\p
\v 13 The¦355516 master¦355515 Yahweh¦355515 says¦355514 that this¦355517 will be¦355518 the¦355518 way that¦355519 you¦355520 divide the¦355523 land¦355523 up for Yisrael's¦355527 twelve tribes¦355526: Yosef¦355528 \add !(Joseph¦355528)\add* will have \add ≈a double allocation\add*.
\v 14 You're to equally divide¦355531 \add the land\add* that¦355535 I¦355536 \add ≈took action\add* to¦355540 give¦355540 to¦355541 your¦355541 ancestors¦355541—\add from now on\add* that land¦355543 will¦355531 come to¦355545 you¦355545 \add Israelis\add* as¦355531 an¦355546 inheritance¦355546.
\p
\v 15 \add #These will be¦355549 the land¦355550 boundaries¦355549\add*: On the north¦355552 side¦355551, it'll extend from¦355553 the¦355555 Mediterranean Sea¦355555 east along the road towards Hetlon¦355558, and¦355548 then¦355548 on to¦355560 Tsedad¦355560,
\v 16 to Berotah¦355564, and then on to Sivrayim¦355565, which¦355566 is¦355567 on the¦355569 border between¦355571 Damascus¦355570 and¦355571 Hamat¦355573. The¦355572 boundary¦355579 will run as far as Hatser¦355574-Hattikon¦355575, which¦355576 is¦355577 on the¦355579 border¦355579 of¦355579 the Havran¦355580 \add region\add*.
\v 17 \add ≈So¦355582\add* the¦355583 boundary¦355583 will extend from¦355584 the¦355586 Mediterranean Sea¦355586 to Hatsar¦355587-Enan¦355588 on the¦355589 border¦355589 between Hamat¦355595 to the north¦355591 and¦355582 Damascus¦355590 \add to the south\add*. \add ≈That'll be¦355582 the northern¦355598 boundary\add*.
\p
\v 18 On¦355621 the eastern side¦355600, the boundary will¦355600 extend between¦355602 Havran¦355603 and¦355604 Damascus¦355606, south along the Yordan¦355612 \add River\add* between the Gilead¦355608 \add region\add* and¦355609 Yisrael's¦355611 territory¦355610, along the Dead Sea¦355616 as far as Tamar.\f + \fr 47:18 \ft Tamar: the Hebrew has ‘the Eastern Sea’.\f* \add ≈That'll be¦355600 the eastern¦355617 boundary\add*.
\p
\v 19 On¦355625 the southern side¦355623, the¦355624 boundary will¦355626 extend from¦355626 Tamar¦355626 to¦355627 the¦355629 springs near Merivat-Kadesh¦355631. Then¦355623 it'll extend \add west\add* along the¦355623 brook of¦355623 Egypt to¦355633 the¦355635 Mediterranean Sea¦355635. \add ≈That'll be¦355626 the southern boundary\add*.
\p
\v 20 On the¦355643 western side¦355643, the boundary¦355648 will be¦355655 the Mediterranean Sea¦355646, \add north\add* to¦355649 a point near Levo¦355652-Hamat¦355653.
\p
\v 21 This¦355663 is how you'll divide¦355659 this¦355663 land¦355662 for¦355664 yourselves among Yisrael's¦355666 tribes¦355665:
\v 22 You must assign the¦355673 land as a permanent possession for yourselves and also¦355673 for¦355673 any foreigners who¦355674 are living¦355674 and raising their children¦355679 among you. You must consider them to¦355672 be like¦355683 native¦355683 born Israelis¦355684, and they¦355681 must be assigned land among¦355684 Yisrael's¦355685 tribes¦355690.
\v 23 Wherever foreigners are¦355693 living, you¦355701 must¦355701 give¦355701 them some land \add ≈to belong to them permanently\add*. That's¦355695 the¦355698 master¦355704 Yahweh's¦355705 declaration¦355703.”
\c 48
\s1 Land allocated to Yisrael's tribes
\rem /s1 Division of the Land; The Division of the Land; The Division of the Land among the Tribes; The Tribal Portions
\p
\v 1 Here's a list of¦355709 Yisrael's tribes¦355710 and the¦355711 territory that each tribe is to¦355713 receive. The¦355715 northern boundary of¦355715 Yisrael will start at the¦355716 Mediterranean Sea and go \add east\add* to Hetlon¦355718 \add city\add*, then to Levo¦355720-Hamat¦355722, and farther on to Hatsar¦355723-Enan¦355724, which is \add south\add* of¦355725 Damascus¦355726, and it'll continue to Hamat. Each tribe will receive land that will extend from¦355711 the eastern¦355737 boundary of Yisrael west to the Mediterranean Sea.
\rem /s1 The Special Section in the Center of the Land
\p The tribe¦355710 of¦355730 Dan¦355739 will¦355732 receive land alongside Yisrael's northern border¦355725.
\v 2 \add ≈South¦355745 of¦355746 their area\add* will be the¦355747 territory for the¦355750 tribe of Asher¦355753.
\v 3 \add ≈South of¦355758\add* Asher's¦355759 land will be territory for the¦355760 tribe of Naftali¦355767.
\v 4 \add ≈South of¦355772\add* Naftali's¦355773 land will be territory for the¦355774 tribe of Menashsheh¦355781.
\v 5 \add ≈South of¦355786\add* Menashsheh's¦355787 land will be territory for the¦355788 tribe of Efrayim¦355795.
\v 6 \add ≈South of¦355800\add* Efrayim's¦355801 land will be territory for the¦355802 tribe of Reuven¦355809.
\v 7 \add ≈South of¦355814\add* Reuven's¦355815 land will be territory for the¦355816 tribe of Yehudah¦355823 \add !(Judah¦355823)\add*.
\s1 The chosen place
\p
\v 8 \add ≈South of¦355827\add* Yehudah's¦355828 land will¦355836 be¦355836 an area that¦355838 the entire¦355840 nation will give to¦355831 me—you \add Israelis\add* will set it¦355836 apart for special use. The¦355827 temple will be¦355841 in¦355857 the¦355857 middle¦355857 of the area that¦355838's thirteen kilometres wide¦355844 and¦355826 as long¦355845 as¦355846 the other land allocations.
\p
\v 9 The¦355863 special¦355859 area that¦355860 you \add Israelis\add* will¦355861 give¦355861 to¦355862 Yahweh¦355862 will¦355864 be¦355864 thirteen kilometres long¦355863 and¦355865 five¦355864 kilometres wide¦355867.
\v 10 This special area will be allocated to¦355871 the priests¦355876: you'll assign them an area of land measuring thirteen kilometres on the northern¦355877 and southern¦355889 sides, and five¦355878 kilometres on the western¦355881 and¦355885 eastern¦355885 sides. Yahweh's¦355897 temple will be in¦355898 the¦355898 middle¦355898 of¦355898 that special area.
\v 11 The¦355901 temple area will be for the priests¦355900, Tsadok's¦355903 descendants¦355902, who are dedicated to¦355900 serving me. (They're¦355913 the ones who served me faithfully and didn't¦355908 turn away \add from me\add* like¦355914 the¦355916 Levites¦355916 \add ≈and the others\add* did.)
\v 12 The¦355922 \add ≈priests' allocation\add* will¦355919 be a portion¦355922 of¦355922 that very sacred¦355922 land¦355923, extending to¦355926 the¦355923 Levites¦355929' border¦355928.
\v 13 The¦355931 land assigned to¦355932 the Levites will be the same size as the land that the priests¦355934 will receive. (So¦355931 together, these two portions of¦355940 land will be thirteen kilometres long and¦355931 about¦355932 ten¦355949 kilometres wide.)
\v 14 They¦355954 mustn't sell¦355954 or barter it¦355955—none of this best part of Yisrael's¦355959 land¦355962 must¦355954 ever be¦355965 \add ≈used for¦355966 any other purpose\add*, because¦355963 it's¦355965 all sacred¦355965 to¦355966 Yahweh¦355966.
\p
\v 15 Another strip of land¦355983 thirteen kilometres long and almost three kilometres wide¦355971 will¦355978 be¦355978 allotted for¦355981 other people living¦355982 in the special area to use. They may build homes and¦355983 have pasturelands there, and¦355984 there'll be¦355984 a city¦355985 in¦355986 the¦355986 middle¦355986 of that¦355970 area¦355978.
\v 16 The¦355991 city \add ≈will¦355993 be¦355993 square, over two kilometres long on each side¦355997\add*,
\v 17 with an open¦356021 area around it¦356020, within the special area, that will be¦356020 about 130m wide in each direction.
\v 18 Outside the city, there'll be two strips that'll extend five kilometres on¦356037 each of¦356042 the east¦356044 and¦356045 west¦356047 sides, and¦356048 the produce¦356053 from \add @those¦356056 strips¦356056\add* will¦356052 be food¦356055 for¦356056 the people working in¦356037 the city¦356057.
\v 19 \add ≈Those¦356064 who¦356059 come from¦356062 the¦356063 various tribes¦356063 to work in the city¦356060 may also work in this farmland\add*.
\v 20 That entire¦356076 special area, including¦356070 the land given for Yahweh's use and¦356070 the city¦356084, will¦356069 be¦356069 a square that's thirteen kilometres long on¦356072 each side.
\p
\v 21 The¦356087 tracts of land to the east and¦356095 to the¦356095 west of¦356095 Yahweh's area and the¦356096 city¦356096 will belong to¦356097 the leader. One area will extend thirteen kilometres east to¦356105 Yisrael's eastern¦356108 boundary, and the other will extend thirteen kilometres west \add to the Mediterranean Sea\add*. Yahweh's area, which¦356086 contains the temple¦356127, will be¦356123 in¦356128 the¦356128 middle¦356128.
\rem /s1 Land for the Other Tribes
\v 22 The¦356132 area that¦356136 belongs¦356137 to the leader (that surrounds the Levites¦356132' property and¦356133 the city¦356134 in¦356135 the¦356135 middle¦356135 of¦356136 it¦356138) will¦356138 be between¦356139 the¦356141 tribe of¦356141 Yehudah¦356142 \add to the north\add* and¦356143 the¦356144 tribe of¦356144 Benyamin¦356145 \add to the south\add*.
\p
\v 23 South of Yahweh's area, each of the¦356150 other tribes¦356150 will receive one¦356159 section of land that extends from¦356151 the eastern¦356152 \add boundary of Yisrael\add* west¦356157 \add all the way to the Mediterranean Sea.\add*
\p Just south of Yahweh's area will be territory for the¦356151 tribe¦356150 of Benyamin¦356158.
\v 24 \add ≈South of¦356163\add* Benyamin's¦356164 land will be the¦356165 land for the¦356169 tribe of Shimeon¦356172.
\v 25 \add ≈South of¦356177\add* Shimeon's¦356178 land will be the¦356179 land for the¦356183 tribe of Yissakar¦356186.
\v 26 \add ≈South of¦356191\add* Yissakar's¦356192 land will be the¦356193 land for the¦356197 tribe of Zevulun¦356200.
\v 27 \add ≈South of¦356205\add* Zevulun's¦356206 land will be the¦356207 land for the¦356211 tribe of Gad¦356214.
\v 28 Gad's¦356219 southern¦356223 boundary will extend from¦356227 Tamar¦356227 \add south\add* to the¦356228 waters¦356228 of¦356228 Merivah¦356229-Kadesh¦356230, and farther \add west\add* to the brook \add of Egypt\add*, and then¦356217 on¦356232 to the¦356234 Mediterranean Sea¦356234.
\p
\v 29 That \add describes\add* the¦356238 territory you'll¦356241 allocate to¦356243 Yisrael's¦356244 tribes¦356243 to become their¦356246 permanent inheritance¦356242. That's the master¦356248 Yahweh's¦356249 declaration¦356247.
\s1 The city gateways
\rem /s1 The Gates of the City; The Gates of Jerusalem
\p
\v 30 These¦356252 will¦356253 be¦356253 the¦356253 exits¦356253 from¦356255 the city¦356254: On the northern¦356256 side¦356255 (which will¦356257 be¦356257 just over two kilometres long),\x + \xo 48:30-34: \xt Rev 21:12-13.\x*
\v 31 there'll be¦356265 three¦356271 gates¦356270 named¦356267 after the¦356263 tribes¦356268 of¦356263 Reuven¦356274, Yehudah¦356277, and¦356263 Levi¦356280.
\v 32 On the¦356285 eastern¦356286 side¦356285 (the same length) there'll be¦356287 three¦356292 gates¦356291 named after the¦356293 tribes of¦356293 Yosef¦356294, Benyamin¦356297, and¦356283 Dan¦356300.
